    <!-- Global (not Astro-scoped): the demo builds rows/prompts at runtime via
         innerHTML, and scoped selectors can't match elements created after
         render. All selectors are page-specific classes, so no bleed. -->
    <style is:global>
      /* Pre-paint fallback: the default palette inlined, so the first frame is
         already the right colours. The theme script then sets `data-bh-theme` and
         styles/themes.css takes over. Keep this in step with `DEFAULT_THEME` in
         src/lib/themes.ts, or the page flashes the old palette on every load.
         Values copied verbatim from the generated registry. */
      :root {
    --bg: #181926;
    --bg2: #1e2030;
    --base: #24273a;
    --surface: #363a4f;
    --line: #494d64;
    --border: #5a5130;
    --overlay0: #6e738d;
    --overlay1: #8087a2;
    --sub: #a5adcb;
    --sub2: #b8c0e0;
    --text: #cad3f5;
    --accent: #dbc66f;
    --sel: #3a3416;
    --green: #94a143;
    --mint: #b8cf6a;
    --amber: #e0a154;
    --coral: #cf5a44;
      }
      * { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; }
      html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
      /* Monospace throughout. This is a terminal program: the page should read
         like one, and a single face across body, headings and code removes the
         sans/mono seam that made the old page feel like a generic template. */
      body {
        background: var(--bg);
        color: var(--text);
        font: 15px/1.75 'Inter Variable', ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
      }
      a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
      /* Prose is the docs' face (`--sl-font`); mono stays where it means
         something: code, and every surface that replicates the running app.
         `.demo` inherits from body, so it is pinned here rather than left to
         chance. */
      code, pre, .term, .demo, .install code {
        font-family: 'JetBrains Mono Variable', ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, monospace;
      }
      /* Display face · the wordmark and headings. Tight tracking is what keeps a
         monospace heading from looking like plain output. */
      h1, h2, h3, .brand, .foot-brand {
        font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, 'JetBrains Mono Variable', monospace;
        line-height: 1.15; letter-spacing: -0.03em; font-weight: 600;
      }
      /* Wide, as before, but the rhythm now comes from generous vertical space
         between sections rather than from cards and shadows. */
      /* The page sits in one frame with hairline rules down both edges, so the
         content has a visible boundary instead of floating in the viewport.
         Sections then run the frame's full width and divide with a rule of
         their own — much more room than a 1080px column, and the lines give
         the extra width something to be measured against. */
      :root { --gut: 2.6rem; }
      /* Touch: no hover, and fingers are not cursors. Reveal what hover would
         otherwise show, and give the demo's small controls a real tap target. */
      @media (hover: none) {
        .fc-go { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
        .demo-tabs .tab, .gtab, .fchip, .ws, .agent, .mc-row { min-height: 2rem; }
        .rs-toggle { min-width: 2.4rem; }
      }

      @media (max-width: 900px) { :root { --gut: 1.6rem; } }
      @media (max-width: 560px) { :root { --gut: 1.15rem; } }
      /* Vertical rules bracket the *content* only. The nav and footer sit
         outside the frame so their horizontal rules run the full viewport
         width, edge to edge, rather than stopping at 1440px. */
      .page-frame {
        max-width: 1440px; margin: 0 auto; background: var(--bg); position: relative;
        border-inline: 1px solid var(--line);
      }
      /* ── Ben-Day dot field ────────────────────────────────────────────────
         A halftone screen, like comic newsprint, on the two sections that are
         *backdrop* rather than content: the hero and the closing call to action.
         Deliberately not the whole page. Behind the feature list and the
         orchestration section it reads as texture inside a card, which is noise;
         behind an opening and a closing it reads as a lit surface.

         **Registration.** Both lattices share one pitch, and the second sits
         exactly half a tile off on *both* axes. Together they form the 45-degree
         staggered screen a real halftone uses. (Two different pitches would beat
         against each other into a moire instead, which is what makes a dot
         background look like a mistake.)

         **The pulse.** The two lattices breathe on *close but unequal* cycles, so
         they slowly beat against each other: sometimes both bright, and the
         screen is dense and high contrast; sometimes opposed, and the dots appear
         to shimmer diagonally between the two registrations; sometimes both low,
         and it settles back. That drift is where the contrast comes from. Locking
         them to exact antiphase was the first attempt and it is worse: the total
         ink stays constant, so nothing ever swells, and the fixed period reads as
         a metronome. Unequal cycles make the field evolve over ~50s without ever
         repeating in an obvious way.

         **Cost.** No DOM, two pseudo-elements per section, and `opacity` is the
         only animated property, so the compositor never repaints or relayouts.
         The mask is static. Writing "a lot of dots" as real elements would be the
         obvious approach and also the slowest.

         Tune from the variables below rather than by editing the gradients. */
      :root {
        --dot-pitch: 20px;   /* one pitch for both lattices; they must register */
        --dot-r: 1.1px;      /* dot radius */
        --dot-alpha: 0.38;   /* ink strength, before the pulse */
        /* Deliberately not multiples of each other: coprime-ish cycles beat
           slowly instead of locking into a visible rhythm. */
        --dot-cycle-a: 9s;
        --dot-cycle-b: 11s;
      }
      .hero::before, .hero::after,
      .cta-repo::before, .cta-repo::after {
        content: ''; position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: -1;
        pointer-events: none;
        /* The accent, so the screen carries the active theme rather than being a
           fixed grey that fights half the palettes. */
        background-image: radial-gradient(
          circle at center,
          rgb(from var(--accent) r g b / var(--dot-alpha)) var(--dot-r),
          transparent calc(var(--dot-r) + 0.7px)
        );
        background-size: var(--dot-pitch) var(--dot-pitch);
        animation: bh-dot-pulse var(--dot-cycle-a) cubic-bezier(0.45, 0, 0.55, 1) infinite;
      }
      /* Half a tile across and down: the offset that makes the pair a screen.
         Its own cycle length is what sets up the beat. */
      .hero::after, .cta-repo::after {
        background-position: calc(var(--dot-pitch) / 2) calc(var(--dot-pitch) / 2);
        animation-duration: var(--dot-cycle-b);
        animation-delay: calc(var(--dot-cycle-b) / -3);
      }
      /* Depth, and the reason the screen stops at the content: each section fades
         its dots out towards the middle, where the copy and the mock sit, and
         keeps them at the edges. Static, so it costs nothing per frame. */
      .hero::before, .hero::after {
        -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(115% 90% at 50% 38%, transparent 16%, #000 82%);
        mask-image: radial-gradient(115% 90% at 50% 38%, transparent 16%, #000 82%);
      }
      /* A wide clear core, so the screen never reaches the card: the dots live in
         the band around it, not under it. */
      .cta-repo::before, .cta-repo::after {
        -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(110% 130% at 50% 50%, transparent 52%, #000 92%);
        mask-image: radial-gradient(110% 130% at 50% 50%, transparent 52%, #000 92%);
      }
      /* Nothing needs lifting above the screen: `z-index: -1` puts it behind every
         child, and the section's own `isolation: isolate` keeps it from sliding
         behind the page background. Enumerating the children that must sit above
         it was the first attempt, and it missed `.demo` in the hero, so the dots
         drew straight over the mock.

         `.demo` and `.cta-card` paint their own backgrounds, so the screen stops
         at their edges rather than showing through them. */
      /* The floor is well above zero: at the bottom of the beat the screen should
         soften, never blink out. */
      /* The floor is what you see most of the time, so it carries the field; the
         peak is the swell. At 0.22 the dim half of the beat multiplied out to
         0.04 alpha, which is invisible, so the screen only existed on paper. */
      @keyframes bh-dot-pulse {
        0%, 100% { opacity: 1; }
        50%      { opacity: 0.45; }
      }
      /* A pulsing background is exactly what this setting is for. Both lattices
         hold at full strength, so the screen stays complete, just still. */
      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
